# moe-phone patch for github.com/Helldez/BigMoeOnEdge @ 74ba18f, applied AFTER 0002-0010.
# --expert-slru: segmented-LRU eviction for the expert cache (probation + protected, O(1)).
#
# WHY. gates/evict_sim.py, on the committed Qwen3 routing trace and in the GLOBAL scope the engine
# actually uses, finds segmented LRU the only deployable policy that beats LRU: +1.42 points of hit rate
# at the phone's cache fraction, 10.8% less miss traffic, and 8-11% across 25-54% cache. Exact frequency
# scoring does better in principle and is NOT used: it needs a scan over candidates at every eviction,
# which would land on the 20 ms/token this engine already spends managing the cache. Plain frequency was
# also measured WORSE than LRU on a long trace (stale popularity pollutes), matching what
# gates/expert_policy.py found per-layer on a different model.
#
# WHAT IT DOES. Entries seen once sit in probation and are what eviction takes. A second sighting
# promotes to a protected list whose cold tail DEMOTES back to probation rather than being dropped, so
# a one-off expert cannot displace a recurring one. Both lists reuse the existing cprev_/cnext_ arrays
# (an entry is in exactly one list), so every operation stays O(1) -- the same cost as the LRU it
# replaces.
#
# THE SUBTLETY THAT COST TWO BUILDS. On the --overlap path touch_entry runs with promote=false and the
# promotion happens later, in the token-major loop that re-orders every touched id. Hooking the policy
# into touch_entry alone changed nothing. Hooking it into that loop still changed nothing, because the
# loop touches entries that MISSED this token too: everything was promoted on first sight, 9176
# promotions produced 8673 immediate demotions, and the hit rate did not move by a thousandth. Promotion
# is now conditioned on seen_[e], the layer's own miss marker, so only a genuine hit promotes.
#
# MEASURED, host, Qwen3-30B-A3B, 1600 MiB cache, 48 tokens: hit 40.6% -> 46.4%, evictions 11037 -> 9890,
# re-read bytes 188.4 -> 164.5 MiB/token. Lossless: the completion text is identical, which is what an
# eviction policy must be -- it changes only which bytes have to be read again.
# ===== engine (BigMoeOnEdge root) =====
